qwen-warp
Copyright (c) 2026 Tuan Nguyen

A single-sequence decode engine (CUDA, W4A16 g128 + MTP speculative decoding, optional 4-bit
KV cache) for the Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27B INT4 checkpoint on one RTX 3090, with an
OpenAI-compatible server.
"Qwen" is a trademark of its owners; this project is not affiliated with them or with UkisAI.

This project's own code and text are released under the MIT License (see LICENSE).

Model weights (not included)
  No weights, tokenizer files or chat templates are distributed here. The engine reads a
  local copy of the Hugging Face checkpoint ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b-INT4 (compressed-tensors
  W4A16 build of ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b), which is under its own licence
  (swift-open-license-1.0, see its model card). Obtain it from its owner and follow its terms;
  this repository's MIT licence does not cover it.

Third-party projects: credits and what was taken
  Provenance was audited by comparing an earlier version of this tree with local clones of the
  upstream repositories (identifiers, comment text, code lines, token sequences). The audit is
  mechanical and cannot exclude a paraphrased idea; it found no copied code.

  bonsai-turbo (RightNow-AI, Apache-2.0): ideas only. Read as prior art for fused decode
    phases, a persistent grid-synchronised decode kernel and a column-parallel gated-DeltaNet
    decode step. No file, function, identifier or comment was copied; our DeltaNet kernel
    is independently written.

  lucebox (Luce-Org, Apache-2.0): ideas only. Read as prior art for a persistent megakernel
    on sm_86, DeltaNet state handling for speculative verification, and streaming weight
    loads with the PTX L1::no_allocate qualifier (a standard PTX form; our asm line is
    independent). No file or fragment was copied.

  open-jet (L-Forster, AGPL-3.0): design reference only; no code copied. Read to understand
    how a fused cooperative-kernel MTP cycle is organised. Spot-check: none of 16 distinctive
    open-jet identifiers occurs in the code of this tree; none of 2,008 open-jet comment lines
    (>= 35 characters) occurs verbatim; the only identical code lines are 28 trivial CUDA/PTX
    one-liners (warp-shuffle sum, lane/warp index, mma.sync operand list). No open-jet file is
    included, so the AGPL does not apply to this repository.

  HyperQwen (syv-ai, Apache-2.0): reference point for measurements only, not a competitor. Its
    public container image was run unmodified on the same hardware: ghcr.io/syv-ai/hyperqwen
    @sha256:00713ba1e04b68db1be9eba2b15b579250f9fb0c29ed61add40c750228fc0cf8, OCI revision
    bd6c5e2f11628483109031d99d1cc4a7d22b1a26, built 2026-10-04 (the project changes quickly).
    None of its patches, scripts or model files are included.

  CUTLASS (NVIDIA, BSD-3-Clause): engine/src/dualgemm/ holds the DualGemm headers of CUTLASS's
    examples/45_dual_gemm (CUTLASS 4.8), copied byte for byte (their licence headers are
    intact; the SiLU*mul epilogue is our own, in engine/src/prefill_swiglu.cu, which uses them
    for the fused gate|up GEMM of the prefill). The rest of CUTLASS is not
    included: the build reads its headers from the flashinfer wheel of the locked environment.

  vLLM (Apache-2.0): used as a library and as the baseline. server/ drives vLLM's AsyncLLM and
    OpenAI frontend through an in-process engine-core client and a plugin; no vLLM file is
    modified or redistributed. engine/prefill.py calls vLLM's bundled FlashAttention-2
    binding and flash-linear-attention ("fla", MIT) at run time.

  vLLM, SGLang (Apache-2.0), llama.cpp (MIT) and Hugging Face transformers (Apache-2.0), read for
    the sampling parameters (presence / frequency / repetition penalty, min_p, logit_bias,
    min_tokens, allowed_token_ids): the order of operations and the semantics follow vLLM's v1
    sampler (vllm/v1/sample), and the reference tests in tests/test_logit_procs.py reuse the
    inputs and expected values of cases in vLLM's tests/v1/sample, SGLang's penaltylib tests and
    llama.cpp's tests/test-sampling.cpp, rewritten for this engine's kernels. The start-up
    memory check follows the idea of vLLM's determine_available_memory (gpu_worker.py) and
    SGLang's kv_cache_configurator. No source file of these projects is included; the
    reference tests call vLLM's own apply_penalties at run time.

  Hugging Face transformers (Apache-2.0) and compressed-tensors (Apache-2.0): the semantics of
    the Qwen3.5 hybrid text path and its MTP head (operation order, shapes, rounding points)
    were transcribed from transformers' qwen3_5 modeling code and vLLM's qwen3_5_mtp model;
    the code is not a copy of that source. engine/weights.py (a safetensors reader for the
    compressed-tensors pack-quantized layout) is implemented independently and was only
    cross-checked against the library. transformers is used at run time for the tokenizer.

  Read for ideas only (nothing taken, nothing redistributed): megakernels-vs-cuda-graphs
    (msaroufim), tpu-megakernels (Inferact, Apache-2.0), pdl-megakernel-reconstruction (MIT),
    MegaQwen (Infatoshi, MIT), Megakernels (HazyResearch, MIT).

KV-cache format: the 4-bit per-token, per-head asymmetric (scale + minimum) layout and its
attention kernels are our own design; asymmetric integer KV quantisation is a standard technique.

Shared idioms: the mma.sync and cp.async inline-asm wrappers and the warp-shuffle reductions
in engine/ are the standard forms prescribed by the NVIDIA PTX ISA, and the lop3 magic-number
nibble unpack is a widely used technique; a few lines coincide textually with other projects.

If you think something is attributed incorrectly or missing, please open an issue.
